
/* =============== 5.2 flashcards & mnemonics =============== */
const PARTN={1:"Part 1 · The disease",2:"Part 2 · Course and diagnosis",3:"Part 3 · Treatment",4:"Part 4 · Living, caring, preventing",5:"Part 5 · History"};
const FC=[
[1,'Is parkinsonism the same thing as Parkinson\'s disease?','No. Parkinsonism is a syndrome with several causes, and Parkinson\'s disease (PD) is one of them. Of 364 new cases of parkinsonism in Olmsted County, Minnesota (1976-1990), 42% were PD, 20% drug-induced, 17% unspecified, 14% parkinsonism in dementia and 7% other causes.'],
[1,'How many people were living with Parkinson\'s disease worldwide, according to WHO?','Over 8.5 million in 2019, and prevalence has doubled in the past 25 years. GBD 2016 counted 6.1 million people with PD in 2016, compared with 2.5 million in 1990.'],
[1,'Is the rise in Parkinson\'s disease explained by ageing alone?','Not entirely. In GBD 2016, crude prevalence rose 74.3% from 1990 to 2016, but age-standardised prevalence also rose, by 21.7%. The authors attribute the rise mainly to more older people, with possible contributions from longer disease duration and environmental factors.'],
[1,'Is Parkinson\'s disease more common in men or women?','In men. The GBD male:female ratio of age-standardised prevalence was 1.40 in 2016. In North America, male:female incidence ratios at age 65 and over ranged from 1.66 to 2.57 across data sources.'],
[1,'How much of the nigra is lost when motor symptoms begin?','Sources disagree. Most reviews say about 50% of nigral dopamine neurons (estimates up to 60-70%). Cheng and colleagues\' reanalysis puts it nearer 30% of neurons but 50-60% of their axon terminals: the \'axons first\' argument.'],
[1,'What are the direct and indirect pathways of the basal ganglia?','The direct pathway is a projection from striatum straight to GPi/SNr, made by D1-receptor medium spiny neurons. The indirect pathway is made by D2-receptor neurons and runs via GPe and the subthalamic nucleus (STN).'],
[1,'In the rate model, what happens when striatal dopamine is lost?','Less D2 activation of indirect neurons means more inhibition of GPe, which disinhibits the STN, which over-drives GPi/SNr. Less D1 activation of direct neurons also disinhibits GPi/SNr. The result is more inhibition of the thalamus and less activation of the cortex.'],
[1,'What are beta oscillations, and why do they matter in Parkinson\'s disease?','Exaggerated synchronised activity in the beta band (13-30 Hz) has been associated with bradykinesia. Both levodopa and STN stimulation reduce it, but it is not known whether they work because they reduce beta or whether the reduction only accompanies the benefit.'],
[1,'What are the three regions of the alpha-synuclein protein?','A 140-amino-acid protein with an amphipathic N-terminal tract (residues 1-60), a central aggregation-prone NAC region (61-95) and a negatively charged C-terminal region (96-140) that protects against aggregation.'],
[1,'What marks the alpha-synuclein in Lewy bodies?','Phosphorylation at serine 129. Under 4% of alpha-synuclein is phosphorylated at S129 in healthy brain, compared with over 90% within Lewy bodies.'],
[1,'What evidence suggests that alpha-synuclein pathology can spread from cell to cell?','Synthetic fibrils injected into the mouse striatum spread pathology through connected regions and killed nigral dopamine neurons (Luk 2012). In people with PD, fetal dopamine neurons grafted 11-16 years earlier, and in another case 14 years earlier, developed Lewy pathology.'],
[1,'What are the six Braak stages?','1: dorsal motor nucleus of IX/X and anterior olfactory nucleus. 2: further lower brainstem, including the caudal raphe and locus coeruleus. 3: substantia nigra and other midbrain and forebrain nuclei. 4: anteromedial temporal mesocortex. 5: high-order association and prefrontal neocortex. 6: first-order association and premotor areas, then primary fields.'],
[1,'Does every brain follow the Braak sequence?','No. In 71 PD brains, 53% fitted caudo-rostral spread and 47% did not; in 7% the vagal nucleus was spared. Across studies, 6.3-43% of cases did not follow the sequence, and there is no relationship between Braak stage and clinical severity.'],
[1,'What is the difference between body-first and brain-first Parkinson\'s disease?','A hypothesis. In body-first PD, pathology starts in the gut or autonomic nerves and climbs the vagus, with REM sleep behaviour disorder and autonomic damage before dopamine loss. In brain-first PD, usually without prodromal RBD, nigrostriatal dysfunction comes before autonomic involvement.'],
[1,'How much of Parkinson\'s disease is genetic?','A 2018 review: about 15% have a family history and 5-10% a monogenic form. Panel-testing studies that include GBA1 risk variants find more: 14.8% in ROPAD and 13% in PD GENEration. The difference is mostly in how GBA1 carriers are counted.'],
[1,'Where is LRRK2 G2019S most common?','Worldwide it is found in 1% of sporadic and 4% of hereditary PD. In North African Arabs it is found in 39% of sporadic and 36% of hereditary PD, and in Ashkenazi Jews in 10% and 28%. In Asians it is under 0.1%.'],
[1,'Why do estimates of LRRK2 G2019S penetrance differ so much?','Published estimates range from 24% to 100%, mainly because of how families were recruited. Family-based estimates reach about 74-80% by age 79, while kin-cohort studies of unselected relatives give about 26-42.5% by age 80.'],
[1,'How is GBA1 linked to Gaucher disease and to Parkinson\'s disease?','GBA1 encodes the lysosomal enzyme glucocerebrosidase. Two disease-causing variants cause Gaucher disease; one variant raises PD risk with reduced penetrance. In Ashkenazi Jews, L444P or N370S was found in 15% of people with PD versus 3% of controls.'],
[2,'How do the 2015 MDS criteria define parkinsonism?','Bradykinesia, in combination with at least one of rest tremor (4-6 Hz) or rigidity. Postural instability is not part of the definition.'],
[2,'What counts as bradykinesia under the MDS criteria?','Slowness of movement AND decrement in amplitude or speed, or progressive hesitations or halts, as movements are continued. Slowness alone is not enough.'],
[2,'What counts as rigidity under the MDS criteria?','\'Lead-pipe\', velocity-independent resistance to slow passive movement of major joints, with the person relaxed. Isolated cogwheeling without lead-pipe rigidity does not meet the requirement.'],
[2,'What are the steps of the MDS 2015 clinical diagnostic criteria?','First, parkinsonism must be present. Second, none of the 9 absolute exclusion criteria may be present. Third, the 10 red flags must be counterbalanced by the 4 supportive criteria.'],
[2,'What separates clinically established from clinically probable Parkinson\'s disease?','Established: no absolute exclusions, at least two supportive criteria and no red flags. Probable: no absolute exclusions, and no more than two red flags, each counterbalanced by a supportive criterion.'],
[2,'How accurate were the MDS criteria in validation studies?','Against expert diagnosis in 626 people, clinically probable PD was met by 94.5% of those with PD, and 88.5% without PD were correctly excluded (accuracy 92.6%). The UK Brain Bank criteria scored 86.4%. Against autopsy, MDS probable PD was 92.5% accurate.'],
[2,'In Hughes 1992, how many of 100 brains diagnosed with Parkinson\'s disease in life showed nigral Lewy bodies?','76. The other 24 included progressive supranuclear palsy, multiple system atrophy, Alzheimer\'s disease and vascular disease. Applying criteria retrospectively raised accuracy to 82%.'],
[2,'What is prodromal Parkinson\'s disease?','The stage when early signs of PD neurodegeneration are present but classic motor PD cannot yet be diagnosed. The MDS research criteria call it \'probable prodromal PD\' at 80% certainty or more, combining an age-based prior with likelihood ratios. They are for research only.'],
[2,'Which marker carries the largest likelihood ratio in the MDS prodromal criteria?','REM sleep behaviour disorder confirmed by polysomnography: LR+ 130 in the 2019 update. Clearly abnormal dopaminergic imaging is 43.3 and olfactory loss 6.4.'],
[2,'How often does isolated REM sleep behaviour disorder convert to a neurodegenerative syndrome?','About 6.25% a year in the International RBD Study Group cohort of 1,280 people. Kaplan-Meier risk was 31.3% at 5 years and 73.5% at 12 years. Of converters, 56.5% developed parkinsonism first and 43.5% dementia first.'],
[2,'In what order do prodromal features appear before diagnosis?','In one prospective cohort: olfactory loss more than 20 years before; colour vision, constipation and erectile dysfunction 10-16 years; urinary problems and subtle cognitive decline 7-9 years; motor examination changes 5-7 years. Bradykinesia came about 5-6, rigidity 3 and tremor 2 years before.'],
[2,'How well does the CSF alpha-synuclein seed amplification assay (SAA) detect Parkinson\'s disease?','In PPMI, sensitivity was 87.7% and specificity in controls 96.3%. It was positive in 98.6% of sporadic PD with smell loss but only 67.5% of LRRK2 PD. The FDA has endorsed it for trial enrolment, not as a diagnostic test.'],
[2,'What are the Hoehn and Yahr stages?','1 unilateral involvement; 2 bilateral without impaired balance; 3 first impaired balance (some postural instability, physically independent); 4 severely disabling disease, still able to walk or stand unassisted; 5 confined to bed or wheelchair unless aided.'],
[2,'How long do people spend in each Hoehn and Yahr stage?','In a Singapore cohort of 695 the median times were: stage 1 to 2, 20 months; 2 to 2.5, 62 months; 2.5 to 3, 25 months; 3 to 4, 24 months; 4 to 5, 26 months. Older age at diagnosis predicted faster transitions.'],
[2,'What did the Sydney Multicentre Study find at 20 years?','74% of 136 people had died. Dementia was present in 83% of survivors, 48% lived in nursing homes and only one person lived independently. The main problems were features that do not respond to levodopa.'],
[2,'What did the CamPaIGN cohort find at 10 years?','55% had died, 68% had postural instability and 46% had dementia. 23% had a \'good outcome\': alive without dementia or postural instability. Pneumonia was the commonest cause of death.'],
[2,'How does essential tremor differ from the tremor of Parkinson\'s disease?','Essential tremor is a bilateral upper-limb action tremor of at least 3 years\' duration, without parkinsonism, dystonia or ataxia; head tremor is common. The MDS PD criteria count only a 4-6 Hz rest tremor; kinetic and postural tremor do not qualify.'],
[2,'Does drug-induced parkinsonism always go away when the drug is stopped?','Usually, within weeks to months, but it persists or progresses in 10-50% of people, who may have had preclinical PD unmasked by the drug. DaT imaging is normal in pure drug-induced parkinsonism.'],
[2,'How is dementia with Lewy bodies told apart from Parkinson\'s disease dementia?','By the \'1-year rule\': dementia before or within about a year of parkinsonism is labelled DLB; dementia in well-established PD is PD dementia. The DLB Consortium supports the rule but calls the cutoff arbitrary and based on expert opinion.'],
[3,'Why is levodopa given with carbidopa?','Carbidopa blocks the decarboxylation of levodopa outside the brain and does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It cuts the levodopa needed for a given response by about 75% and makes nausea and vomiting less frequent.'],
[3,'What is the plasma half-life of levodopa?','About 50 minutes without carbidopa and about 1.5 hours with it, per the Sinemet label. Other labels report values around 1.5 to 2.3 hours, depending on product and method.'],
[3,'How does dietary protein affect levodopa?','Large neutral amino acids compete with levodopa for transport in the gut and at the blood-brain barrier. In Nutt 1984, high-protein meals reversed the benefit of intravenous levodopa without lowering plasma levodopa, which places the competition at the barrier as well.'],
[3,'What is the difference between the long-duration and the short-duration response to levodopa?','Early on, people notice a sustained benefit with little dose-to-dose change: the long-duration response. On average after 2-5 years, the short-duration response becomes more important, with benefit rising and falling around each dose.'],
[3,'What is wearing-off?','The predictable return of symptoms at the end of a dose. It is the most frequent type of motor fluctuation. Unpredictable \'sudden OFFs\' can also occur, regardless of dose timing.'],
[3,'How does peak-dose dyskinesia differ from diphasic dyskinesia?','Peak-dose dyskinesia coincides with the peak of levodopa action; chorea is the commonest form. Diphasic dyskinesia, usually in 15-20% of people, appears as levels rise and fall (the dystonia-improvement-dystonia pattern) and mostly affects the legs.'],
[3,'How common are motor complications on levodopa?','In a pooled literature analysis, fluctuations were nil in the first year and affected about 40% by 4-6 years; dyskinesia was slightly less than 40%. A 2017 review cites 80-100% after 10 years of dopaminergic therapy.'],
[3,'What did ELLDOPA and LEAP show about levodopa and disease progression?','ELLDOPA: after a 2-week washout, UPDRS had worsened by 7.8 points on placebo versus 1.9, 1.9 and -1.4 on the three levodopa doses, although dopamine-transporter uptake declined more on levodopa in its imaging substudy. LEAP, a delayed-start trial, found no disease-modifying effect of levodopa.'],
[3,'What did the agonist-first trials show?','Fewer motor complications but less motor benefit. In CALM-PD, motor complications at 23.5 months were 28% with pramipexole versus 51% with levodopa. In the 056 study, dyskinesia at 5 years was 20% with ropinirole versus 45% with levodopa.'],
[3,'How common are impulse-control disorders on dopamine agonists?','In DOMINION (3,090 people), 17.1% on an agonist versus 6.9% not on one. In the DIGPD cohort, 5-year cumulative incidence was 51.5% in agonist ever-users versus 12.4% in never-users, and disorders gradually resolved after agonists were stopped.'],
[3,'How do COMT inhibitors change levodopa levels?','Entacapone 200 mg raises levodopa exposure by about 35% and lengthens its half-life from 1.3 to 2.4 hours, without much change in the peak. It has no antiparkinsonian effect of its own and is given with each levodopa dose.'],
[3,'What does NICE NG71 say about first-line treatment?','Offer levodopa when motor symptoms affect quality of life. When they do not, consider a choice of dopamine agonist, levodopa or MAO-B inhibitor. NICE frames the choice around quality of life, not age.'],
[3,'Which three brain targets are used for deep-brain stimulation in Parkinson\'s disease?','The subthalamic nucleus (STN) and the internal globus pallidus (GPi) for the main motor symptoms, and the ventral intermediate thalamus (Vim) for tremor.'],
[3,'What did the main randomised DBS trials find?','Weaver 2009: 4.6 hours a day more on-time without troubling dyskinesia, versus 0 with best medical therapy. EARLYSTIM: quality of life (PDQ-39) improved 7.8 points with STN stimulation and worsened 0.2 with medical therapy.'],
[3,'What is adaptive DBS?','Closed-loop stimulation that adjusts itself to a brain signal. In ADAPT-PD the signal was alpha-beta (8-30 Hz) local field power. Medtronic\'s BrainSense Adaptive DBS, the first commercial system, received FDA approval in February 2025.'],
[3,'What are the main surgical risks of DBS?','In a review of 13,056 patients, intracranial haemorrhage occurred in 2.9% per patient, half of them symptomatic, and all-cause mortality was 0.4%. Surgical-site infection occurred in 4.9% of procedures, mostly at the pulse-generator pocket.'],
[3,'What do the infusion therapies do?','They deliver levodopa or apomorphine continuously. Intestinal levodopa gel cut off-time by 4.04 hours versus 2.14 hours with oral levodopa in a 12-week trial. Subcutaneous foslevodopa-foscarbidopa cut off-time by 2.75 versus 0.96 hours.'],
[3,'Is the \'narrowing therapeutic window\' of levodopa proven?','It is widely taught but disputed. In 20 people followed over 4 years, dyskinesia and the antiparkinsonian response began and ended together, which the authors judged not consistent with a falling dyskinesia threshold. No source gave numeric window widths.'],
[4,'How common are non-motor symptoms in Parkinson\'s disease?','In the PRIAMO study of 1,072 people, 98.6% reported at least one, with a mean of 7.8 each. The commonest were fatigue (58%) and anxiety (56%).'],
[4,'How common are depression, anxiety and apathy?','Major depressive disorder 17% and clinically significant depressive symptoms 35%; anxiety disorders 31%; apathy 39.8%. About half of people with apathy had neither depression nor cognitive impairment, which supports apathy as a separate syndrome.'],
[4,'How common are psychosis and dementia over time?','In a Norwegian cohort, 60% developed hallucinations or delusions over 12 years. Dementia affected 24.5% in a systematic review, and 78.2% at 8 years in another Norwegian cohort.'],
[4,'How is orthostatic hypotension defined?','A sustained fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt. A heart-rate rise under 15 bpm during the fall suggests a neurogenic cause. In supine hypertension a 30/15 mmHg fall may be more appropriate.'],
[4,'How does the MDS 2019 review rate medicines for Parkinson\'s disease psychosis?','Clozapine and pimavanserin: efficacious and clinically useful (clozapine needs specialised monitoring). Quetiapine: insufficient evidence but possibly useful. Olanzapine: not efficacious, unacceptable risk, not useful.'],
[4,'What did the SPARX and Park-in-Shape exercise trials show?','SPARX: high-intensity treadmill exercise changed UPDRS motor scores by 0.3 points in 6 months, versus 3.2 in wait-list controls. Park-in-Shape: home aerobic cycling beat stretching by 4.2 points on the off-state motor score.'],
[4,'How often do swallowing problems go unreported?','Often. Asked directly, 35% reported dysphagia; on objective testing, 82% had it, against 23% of controls.'],
[4,'How common are falls in Parkinson\'s disease?','In 22 prospective studies, 60.5% fell at least once and 39% fell recurrently. Recurrent fallers averaged 20.8 falls a year. Previous falls is the factor most consistently linked to recurrent falls.'],
[4,'Why are Parkinson\'s medicines called time-critical?','Parkinson\'s UK states that \'a delay as little as 30 minutes\' can make the difference between functioning well and being unable to move, walk, talk or swallow. NICE\'s quality standard is levodopa within 30 minutes of the prescribed time; the Parkinson\'s Foundation says within plus or minus 15 minutes.'],
[4,'What can happen if Parkinson\'s medicines are stopped suddenly?','A syndrome resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome (parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia syndrome): rigidity, fever and reduced consciousness, with reported mortality up to 4%. NICE says the medicines should not be withdrawn abruptly or allowed to fail through poor absorption.'],
[4,'Which anti-sickness drugs do the Parkinson\'s organisations list to avoid?','Metoclopramide and prochlorperazine (both lists), plus promethazine and droperidol (Parkinson\'s Foundation). Parkinson\'s UK says domperidone may be considered if there are no contraindications, but not in cardiac disease.'],
[4,'Why is smoking linked to lower Parkinson\'s risk?','Ever smokers had a relative risk of 0.59 in a meta-analysis. A Danish study found that people who found quitting easier were more likely to develop PD, and the authors read \'ease of quitting\' as an early sign of PD rather than proof that tobacco protects.'],
[4,'Which environmental exposures are linked to higher Parkinson\'s risk?','Pesticides (rotenone OR 2.5, paraquat OR 2.5), the solvent trichloroethylene (OR 6.1 in twins, with wide confidence intervals), and contaminated water at Camp Lejeune (OR 1.70). Head injury with concussion gave a pooled OR of 1.57.'],
[4,'Did raising urate or blocking calcium channels slow Parkinson\'s disease?','No. SURE-PD3 (inosine to raise urate) stopped early for futility despite a 44% rise in urate. STEADY-PD III (isradipine) showed no effect on progression. Both had been chosen because of observational associations.'],
[4,'Is there an approved treatment that slows Parkinson\'s disease?','No. The 2015-2024 pipeline review concludes \'there are still no treatments to modify the course of the disease\'. Antibodies, GLP-1 drugs, LRRK2 and GBA1 drugs, cells and gene therapies are all still research.'],
[4,'What happened with Japan\'s stem-cell product for Parkinson\'s disease?','In March 2026 Japan granted conditional, time-limited approval to an iPSC-derived dopamine-cell product (raguneprocel) on the basis of a 7-patient trial. The sponsor has 7 years to gather confirmatory data, and the approval applies only in Japan.'],
[4,'What did the outpatient palliative care trial show?','In 210 people with Parkinson\'s and related disorders, integrated palliative care improved quality of life at 6 months. Caregiver burden did not differ at 6 months but was lower at 12 months.'],
[4,'What did the trials of Parkinson\'s disease nurse specialists find?','Mixed results. In England, community nurse specialists did not change mortality, two timed function tests or PDQ-39, but self-rated global health was better. In a US trial, nurse-led care management raised adherence to quality-of-care indicators (0.77 vs 0.58).'],
[5,'When and where did James Parkinson publish his essay?','\'An Essay on the Shaking Palsy\' was printed in London in 1817. He described six cases, several of them people seen casually in the street.'],
[5,'What did James Parkinson believe about the mind in the shaking palsy?','He wrote that \'the senses and intellects\' were uninjured. Later cohorts contradict this: in the Sydney study, 83% of 20-year survivors had dementia.'],
[5,'Where did James Parkinson place the cause of the disease?','In the upper cervical spinal cord (\'medulla spinalis\'), extending to the medulla oblongata. He did not describe rigidity or bradykinesia as cardinal features.'],
[5,'Which non-motor features did James Parkinson already describe?','Disturbed sleep, \'torpid\' bowels, drooling, difficulty chewing and swallowing, and late loss of speech and incontinence.'],
[5,'What did Charcot add?','Lecturing at the Salpêtrière from the 1870s, he added bradykinesia and rigidity, separated the disease from multiple sclerosis and other tremor disorders, and named it after Parkinson, noting that people are not markedly weak. He used belladonna alkaloids such as hyoscyamine.'],
[5,'Who described Lewy bodies, and who named them?','Fritz Heinrich Lewy described the inclusions in 1912. Konstantin Tretiakoff\'s Paris thesis (1919) described loss of pigmented nigral neurons and named the inclusions \'corps de Lewy\'. Goetz 2011 dates Tretiakoff\'s work to 1921.'],
[5,'What did Arvid Carlsson show?','In 1957-58, reserpine depleted brain monoamines and stopped spontaneous movement in animals, and L-dopa reversed this while a serotonin precursor did not. He shared the 2000 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Paul Greengard and Eric Kandel.'],
[5,'Who first found the dopamine deficit in the Parkinson\'s brain?','Ehringer and Hornykiewicz (1960) found striatal dopamine depletion. In 1961 Birkmayer and Hornykiewicz gave intravenous levodopa to people with PD for the first time.'],
[5,'Which 1967 paper launched high-dose oral levodopa?','Cotzias, Van Woert and Schiffer, \'Aromatic amino acids and modification of parkinsonism\', NEJM, 16 February 1967.'],
[5,'When did the FDA approve levodopa and carbidopa-levodopa?','Levodopa (Larodopa) on 4 June 1970, and carbidopa-levodopa (Sinemet) on 2 May 1975.'],
[5,'What did MPTP teach about Parkinson\'s disease?','In 1983 Langston and colleagues described four people who developed marked parkinsonism after injecting an illicit pethidine (meperidine)-analogue product containing MPTP, which they proposed selectively damages substantia nigra cells.'],
[5,'Where did modern deep-brain stimulation begin?','In 1987 Benabid and colleagues reported that high-frequency (100 Hz) stimulation of the thalamic VIM nucleus stopped parkinsonian tremor. The FDA approved thalamic stimulation for tremor in 1997 and STN or GPi stimulation for PD in 2002.'],
[5,'Why is World Parkinson\'s Day on 11 April, and why the red tulip?','11 April is James Parkinson\'s birthday. The \'Dr James Parkinson\' tulip was bred in 1980 by Dutch horticulturist J.W.S. Van der Wereld, who had PD, and the red tulip became the symbol of PD in 2005. Some pages give 1981 for the tulip.'],
[5,'Where was rasagiline developed?','At the Technion in Israel, by Moussa Youdim and John Finberg in the 1970s, and then by Teva as Azilect. Israel\'s Ministry of Health licensed it on 3 January 2005, and the FDA approved it on 16 May 2006.'],
[5,'What happened in Parkinson\'s research in 1997?','The first PD gene mutation, SNCA A53T, was found in an Italian kindred and Greek families, and Lewy bodies were shown to contain alpha-synuclein (Spillantini and colleagues).']
];
const MN=[
['TRAP: the four classic signs','<b>T</b>remor, <b>R</b>igidity, <b>A</b>kinesia (bradykinesia), <b>P</b>ostural instability. This is the classic teaching mnemonic. The MDS 2015 criteria are narrower: parkinsonism is <b>bradykinesia plus rest tremor and/or rigidity</b>, and postural instability is no longer part of the definition (the older UK Brain Bank criteria included it). A memory aid, not a diagnostic tool.'],
['MDS criteria: Gate, 9, 10, 4','<b>Gate</b>: parkinsonism must be present. <b>9</b> absolute exclusions: any one rules PD out. <b>10</b> red flags, weighed against <b>4</b> supportive criteria. <b>Established</b> = 0 exclusions, 0 red flags, at least 2 supportive. <b>Probable</b> = 0 exclusions and no more than 2 red flags, each matched by a supportive criterion. A memory aid; the criteria were built for research.'],
['Braak 1 to 6: up the brainstem, then out across the cortex','<b>1</b> medulla (vagal nucleus) and olfactory nucleus, <b>2</b> more lower brainstem (caudal raphe, locus coeruleus), <b>3</b> midbrain (substantia nigra), <b>4</b> mesocortex (anteromedial temporal), <b>5</b> association neocortex, <b>6</b> primary fields. Roughly: 1-2 before symptoms, 3-4 motor, 5-6 often cognitive. A memory aid: many brains do not follow the sequence, and stage does not track clinical severity.'],
['D1 Direct = Do, D2 inDirect = Don\'t','Dopamine makes transmission <b>easier</b> onto D1 <b>direct</b>-pathway neurons (which release the thalamus, favouring movement) and <b>harder</b> onto D2 <b>indirect</b>-pathway neurons (GPe, then STN, which brake it). Lose dopamine and the brake wins. A memory aid for the rate model, which has known limits.'],
['Carbidopa Can\'t Cross','<b>Carbidopa</b> stays outside the brain and blocks peripheral dopa decarboxylase, so more levodopa reaches the brain: about <b>75%</b> less levodopa is needed, and decarboxylase is saturated at about <b>70-100 mg</b> of carbidopa a day. Levodopa half-life rises from about <b>50 minutes</b> to about <b>1.5 hours</b>. Protein competes with levodopa in the gut and at the blood-brain barrier. A memory aid.'],
['Dominant SLV, recessive PPD','<b>Dominant</b>: <b>S</b>NCA, <b>L</b>RRK2, <b>V</b>PS35. <b>Recessive</b> (often early onset): <b>P</b>RKN (parkin), <b>P</b>INK1, <b>D</b>J-1 (PARK7). <b>GBA1</b> sits apart as a risk gene with reduced penetrance. Together these are the seven genes adjudicated as causal contributors to PD. A memory aid.'],
['Hoehn and Yahr 1 to 5: One side, Both sides, Balance, Bad but walks, Bed or chair','<b>1</b> unilateral, <b>2</b> bilateral without impaired balance, <b>3</b> balance affected but physically independent, <b>4</b> severe disability, still walks or stands unaided, <b>5</b> wheelchair or bed unless aided. The MDS Task Force recommends the original five-point scale; the 0.5-step version has no clinimetric data. A memory aid.'],
['The prodromal clock: Smell 20, Gut 10-16, Bladder 7-9, Motor 5-7','Years before diagnosis in one prospective RBD cohort: smell loss more than <b>20</b>; colour vision, constipation and erectile dysfunction <b>10-16</b>; urinary problems and subtle cognitive decline <b>7-9</b>; motor changes <b>5-7</b>. Then the cardinal signs: bradykinesia about <b>5-6</b>, rigidity <b>3</b>, tremor <b>2</b>. A memory aid; timings come from one cohort.'],
['Motor complications: wear, peak, D-I-D','<b>Wearing-off</b>: symptoms return at the end of a dose (the commonest fluctuation). <b>Peak-dose</b> dyskinesia: at the top of the levodopa curve, usually chorea. <b>D-I-D</b> (diphasic): dystonia, improvement, dystonia, as levels rise and fall, mostly in the legs. <b>Off-period dystonia</b>: often painful, especially in the legs. A memory aid.'],
['Drugs to avoid: block, mix, apo','<b>Block</b>: dopamine blockers such as haloperidol and other typical antipsychotics, metoclopramide and prochlorperazine, which can worsen Parkinson\'s. <b>Mix</b>: rasagiline is contraindicated with pethidine (meperidine), tramadol, methadone, St John\'s wort, cyclobenzaprine and dextromethorphan. <b>Apo</b>: with apomorphine, ondansetron and other 5HT3 antagonists are contraindicated. Sources differ on details. A memory aid, not a prescribing tool.'],
['Time-critical: 30 in the UK, 15 in the US, never zero','NICE quality standard: levodopa within <b>30</b> minutes of the prescribed time in hospital or care home; Parkinson\'s UK wants doses more than 30 minutes late reported as adverse events. The Parkinson\'s Foundation says within <b>plus or minus 15</b> minutes of the home schedule. <b>Never zero</b>: NICE says Parkinson\'s medicines should not be withdrawn abruptly, because of the risk of a neuroleptic malignant-like syndrome. A memory aid.']
];
inits.review=()=>{
  let filt='all',deck=[],i=0;
  const F=[['all','%t:cards.all%'],...Object.keys(PARTN).map(k=>[k,'%t:part%'+k])];
  $('#fcFilter').innerHTML=F.map(([k,l])=>`<button class="btn" data-ff="${k}" aria-pressed="${k==='all'}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  const fc=$('#fc');
  function build(shuf){deck=FC.filter(c=>filt==='all'||String(c[0])===filt);if(shuf){const r=rng(Date.now()%1e6);for(let k=deck.length-1;k>0;k--){const j=Math.floor(r()*(k+1));[deck[k],deck[j]]=[deck[j],deck[k]]}}i=0;render()}
  function render(){const c=deck[i];fc.classList.remove('flip');
    $('#fcTagF').textContent=PARTN[c[0]]+'%t:cards.question%';$('#fcTagB').textContent=PARTN[c[0]]+'%t:cards.answer%';
    $('#fcFront').textContent=c[1];$('#fcBack').textContent=c[2];$('#fcCount').textContent=`${i+1}%t:cards.of%${deck.length}`;$('#fcBar').style.width=((i+1)/deck.length*100)+'%'}
  const flip=()=>fc.classList.toggle('flip');
  fc.addEventListener('click',flip);$('#fcFlip').onclick=flip;
  const next=()=>{i=(i+1)%deck.length;render()},prev=()=>{i=(i-1+deck.length)%deck.length;render()};
  $('#fcNext').onclick=next;$('#fcPrev').onclick=prev;$('#fcShuf').onclick=()=>build(true);
  $('#fcFilter').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-ff]');if(!b)return;filt=b.dataset.ff;$$('[data-ff]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===b));build(false)});
  document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(cur!=='review')return;if(e.target.closest('input')||(e.target.closest('button')&&e.target!==fc))return;
    if(e.key===' '||e.key==='Enter'){e.preventDefault();flip()}else if(e.key==='ArrowRight')next();else if(e.key==='ArrowLeft')prev()});
  build(false);
  $('#mnList').innerHTML=MN.map(m=>`<div class="card"><b class="big">${m[0]}</b><p>${m[1]}</p></div>`).join('');
};

/* =============== 5.3 quiz =============== */
const QZ=[
[1,'Of 364 new cases of parkinsonism in Olmsted County (1976-1990), what share was Parkinson\'s disease?',['About 90%','42%','20%','7%'],1,'42% were PD, 20% drug-induced parkinsonism, 17% unspecified, 14% parkinsonism in dementia and 7% other causes.'],
[1,'In GBD 2016, crude prevalence of Parkinson\'s disease rose 74.3% from 1990 to 2016. What happened to age-standardised prevalence?',['It fell by 10%','It stayed flat','It rose by 21.7%','It rose by 74.3%'],2,'Age-standardised prevalence rose 21.7%, so the increase is not explained by ageing alone. The authors attribute it mainly to more older people, with possible roles for longer disease duration and environmental factors.'],
[1,'How many people with Parkinson\'s disease do Su and colleagues (BMJ 2025) project for 2050?',['6.1 million','8.5 million','12 million','25.2 million'],3,'25.2 million, a 112% increase from 2021, with 89% of the growth attributed to ageing. Dorsey and colleagues had projected over 12 million by 2040; the projections use different GBD rounds and assumptions.'],
[1,'Which pathway is formed by D1-receptor medium spiny neurons projecting straight to GPi/SNr?',['The direct pathway','The indirect pathway','The nigrostriatal pathway','The mesocortical pathway'],0,'The direct pathway. The indirect pathway is formed by D2-receptor neurons and runs via GPe and the STN.'],
[1,'In the basal ganglia rate model, what happens to the subthalamic nucleus when dopamine is lost?',['It falls silent','It is disinhibited and over-drives GPi/SNr','It switches to D1 receptors','It stops projecting to the pallidum'],1,'Less D2 activation leads to more inhibition of GPe, which disinhibits the STN and drives GPi/SNr, so the thalamus is more inhibited. MPTP monkeys show more STN and GPi activity, as the model predicts.'],
[1,'Exaggerated synchronisation in which frequency band has been associated with bradykinesia?',['Delta (1-4 Hz)','Theta (5-7 Hz)','Beta (13-30 Hz)','Gamma (about 70 Hz)'],2,'Beta (13-30 Hz). Levodopa reduced this low-frequency activity and produced a new peak near 70 Hz. Whether the reduction causes the benefit is unresolved.'],
[1,'Which region of alpha-synuclein is the aggregation-prone part?',['The N-terminal tract (1-60)','The central NAC region (61-95)','The C-terminal tail (96-140)','The signal peptide'],1,'The central NAC region is aggregation-prone. The negatively charged C-terminal region protects against aggregation, and the N-terminal tract has alpha-helical tendency.'],
[1,'What share of alpha-synuclein in Lewy bodies is phosphorylated at serine 129?',['Under 4%','About 25%','About 50%','Over 90%'],3,'Over 90%, compared with under 4% in healthy brain. Single phosphorylation at Ser129 is the main modification of alpha-synuclein in Lewy bodies.'],
[1,'Which observation in people with Parkinson\'s disease supports host-to-graft spread of pathology?',['Grafted fetal neurons developed Lewy bodies after 11-16 years','Grafts formed tumours','Grafts never survived','Grafts cured the disease'],0,'Two people whose fetal dopamine grafts had survived 11-16 years had Lewy bodies in the grafted cells, and another had Lewy-like inclusions after 14 years. Most grafted cells still appeared functional after a decade.'],
[1,'Where do Braak stage 1 lesions start?',['Substantia nigra','Transentorhinal cortex','Dorsal motor nucleus of IX/X and anterior olfactory nucleus','Primary motor cortex'],2,'Stage 1 involves the dorsal motor nucleus of the glossopharyngeal and vagal nerves and the anterior olfactory nucleus. The substantia nigra is reached at stage 3.'],
[1,'In 71 Parkinson\'s brains studied by Kalaitzakis and colleagues, what share fitted caudo-rostral spread?',['100%','83%','53%','17%'],2,'53% fitted and 47% did not, and in 7% the vagal nucleus was spared despite nigral and cortical inclusions. Across studies there is no relationship between Braak stage and clinical severity.'],
[1,'In the body-first hypothesis, what comes before dopaminergic involvement?',['Unilateral rest tremor','Cortical dementia','Normal cardiac MIBG imaging','REM sleep behaviour disorder and autonomic damage'],3,'Body-first PD is proposed to start in the gut or autonomic nerves, with prodromal RBD and autonomic damage first. Brain-first PD is usually RBD-negative, with nigrostriatal dysfunction first.'],
[1,'In the ROPAD study, what share of 12,580 people with Parkinson\'s disease had a PD-relevant positive genetic result?',['1.5%','5%','14.8%','40%'],2,'14.8%, most of them GBA1 risk variants (10.4%) and LRRK2 (2.9%). The older \'5-10% monogenic\' figure excludes most GBA1 carriers.'],
[1,'In which group is LRRK2 G2019S most common among people with Parkinson\'s disease?',['Asians','British','German/Austrian','North African Arabs'],3,'In North African Arabs it was found in 39% of sporadic and 36% of hereditary PD, and in Ashkenazi Jews in 10% and 28%. In Asians it is under 0.1%.'],
[1,'Why do published LRRK2 G2019S penetrance estimates range from 24% to 100%?',['Differences in how families were recruited, plus age, sex, ancestry and modifiers','Laboratory error','The mutation changes over a lifetime','Different genes were tested'],0,'Family-based estimates (about 74-80% by age 79) far exceed kin-cohort estimates of unselected relatives (about 26-42.5% by age 80).'],
[1,'What does carrying one disease-causing GBA1 variant do?',['Causes Gaucher disease','Raises Parkinson\'s risk with reduced penetrance','Always causes Parkinson\'s by age 50','Protects against Parkinson\'s'],1,'Two variants cause Gaucher disease; one raises PD risk with reduced penetrance. Published penetrance by age 80 ranges from about 8% to 30%, depending on how carriers were found.'],
[1,'In early-onset families studied by Lücking and colleagues, parkin mutations explained 77% of isolated cases with onset at what age?',['20 years or younger','30-40 years','50-60 years','Over 70 years'],0,'77% (10 of 13) of isolated cases with onset at 20 or younger, but only 3% with onset after 30. Parkin carriers often had dystonia at onset and responded well to levodopa.'],
[1,'About how much of Parkinson\'s liability is estimated to come from common genetic variation?',['About 2%','About 22%','About 60%','About 90%'],1,'About 22% (the 2019 meta-GWAS gave 0.22, 95% CI 0.18-0.26). The 90 risk signals found explained 16-36% of heritable risk.'],
[2,'How do the 2015 MDS criteria define parkinsonism?',['Rest tremor alone','Rigidity plus postural instability','Bradykinesia plus rest tremor and/or rigidity','Any two of tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia and postural instability'],2,'Bradykinesia in combination with at least one of rest tremor (4-6 Hz) or rigidity. Postural instability is not part of the MDS definition.'],
[2,'Which tremor counts toward parkinsonism under the MDS criteria?',['Kinetic tremor','Postural tremor','Isolated head tremor','4-6 Hz tremor in a fully resting limb, suppressed when movement starts'],3,'Only rest tremor qualifies. Kinetic and postural tremor do not count toward the parkinsonism criteria.'],
[2,'Which combination gives \'clinically established\' Parkinson\'s disease?',['No exclusions, at least two supportive criteria, no red flags','No exclusions and one red flag','Two exclusions balanced by two supportive criteria','Any levodopa response'],0,'Established PD needs all three. The design target was that at least 90% of people meeting it truly have PD.'],
[2,'How many red flags are allowed for \'clinically probable\' Parkinson\'s disease?',['None','No more than two, each counterbalanced by a supportive criterion','Up to five','Any number if levodopa works'],1,'One red flag needs at least one supportive criterion and two need at least two; no more than two red flags are allowed in this category.'],
[2,'Which of these is an MDS absolute exclusion criterion?',['Rest tremor of a limb','Olfactory loss','Levodopa-induced dyskinesia','Downward vertical supranuclear gaze palsy'],3,'Downward vertical supranuclear gaze palsy or selective slowing of downward saccades is an exclusion. The other three are supportive criteria.'],
[2,'Which of these is an MDS supportive criterion?',['Bilateral symmetric parkinsonism','Olfactory loss or cardiac sympathetic denervation on MIBG','A normal DaT scan','Early severe bulbar dysfunction'],1,'Olfactory loss or MIBG denervation is supportive. Symmetric onset and early bulbar dysfunction are red flags, and normal presynaptic dopaminergic imaging is an absolute exclusion.'],
[2,'In Hughes 1992, how many of 100 brains diagnosed with Parkinson\'s disease by neurologists had nigral Lewy bodies?',['76','50','95','100'],0,'76. The other 24 included PSP, MSA, Alzheimer\'s disease and vascular disease. A later meta-analysis put pooled clinical accuracy at 80.6%, with no improvement over 25 years.'],
[2,'In the MDS prodromal criteria, \'probable prodromal Parkinson\'s disease\' means at least what certainty?',['50%','65%','80%','99%'],2,'At least 80%, estimated by multiplying an age-based prior by likelihood ratios. The criteria are for research only.'],
[2,'Which marker has a positive likelihood ratio of 130 in the 2019 MDS prodromal update?',['Constipation','Olfactory loss','Depression','REM sleep behaviour disorder confirmed by polysomnography'],3,'PSG-proven RBD, LR+ 130. Olfactory loss is 6.4, constipation 2.5 and depression with or without anxiety 1.6.'],
[2,'In the International RBD Study Group cohort, what was the 12-year risk of conversion to a neurodegenerative syndrome?',['10.6%','31.3%','51.4%','73.5%'],3,'73.5% at 12 years (31.3% at 5 years), about 6.25% a year, with a median of 8 years. Some converted to dementia first and some to parkinsonism first.'],
[2,'Which prodromal feature is predicted to start earliest, more than 20 years before diagnosis?',['Olfactory loss','Rest tremor','Rigidity','Urinary dysfunction'],0,'Olfactory loss. Constipation and erectile dysfunction start 10-16 years before, urinary problems 7-9, and tremor only about 2 years before diagnosis.'],
[2,'In PPMI, the CSF alpha-synuclein seed amplification assay was positive in what share of LRRK2 Parkinson\'s disease?',['67.5%','87.7%','98.6%','4%'],0,'67.5%, against 98.6% in sporadic PD with smell loss and 87.7% overall. About a third of LRRK2 PD lacks Lewy pathology at autopsy too.'],
[2,'What is the US regulatory status of the alpha-synuclein seed amplification assay?',['FDA-approved diagnostic test','Banned','An FDA Letter of Support for use as a risk biomarker in clinical trials','Approved only for Alzheimer\'s disease'],2,'The FDA endorsed CSF SAA as a susceptibility and risk biomarker for trial enrolment, which is not a diagnostic approval. The Syn-One skin test is a laboratory assay not approved by the FDA as a diagnostic test.'],
[2,'Which Hoehn and Yahr stage marks the first impairment of balance, with the person still physically independent?',['Stage 1','Stage 2','Stage 3','Stage 5'],2,'Stage 3. Stage 2 is bilateral without impaired balance, stage 4 severe disability but still able to walk or stand unassisted, stage 5 wheelchair or bed unless aided.'],
[2,'In the Sydney Multicentre Study, what share of 20-year survivors had dementia?',['12%','35%','56%','83%'],3,'83%. 74% of the cohort had died, and falls (87%) and freezing (81%) were common in survivors.'],
[2,'Drug-induced parkinsonism usually resolves after the drug is stopped. In what share does it persist or progress?',['None','10-50%','About 90%','100%'],1,'10-50%, possibly because preclinical PD was unmasked. DaT imaging is normal in pure drug-induced parkinsonism.'],
[2,'Which MRI sign had 99.5% specificity for progressive supranuclear palsy?',['Hot-cross-bun sign','Swallow-tail sign','Hummingbird sign','Eye-of-the-tiger sign'],2,'The hummingbird sign (midbrain atrophy): specificity 99.5% but sensitivity only 51.6%. The hot-cross-bun sign is an MRI marker in the MSA criteria.'],
[2,'How does the DLB Consortium separate dementia with Lewy bodies from Parkinson\'s disease dementia?',['With an amyloid PET scan','By the 1-year rule','With a genetic test','By the levodopa response'],1,'The consortium remains supportive of the 1-year rule but calls the cutoff arbitrary and based on expert opinion.'],
[3,'Why is carbidopa given with levodopa?',['It turns levodopa into dopamine inside the brain','It blocks peripheral decarboxylation and does not cross the blood-brain barrier','It is a dopamine agonist','It blocks COMT in the brain'],1,'Carbidopa blocks dopa decarboxylase outside the brain, so about 75% less levodopa is needed and nausea is less frequent. It does not affect levodopa metabolism in the brain.'],
[3,'According to the Sinemet label, what is levodopa\'s plasma half-life when given with carbidopa?',['About 10 minutes','About 1.5 hours','About 12 hours','About 3 days'],1,'About 1.5 hours, against about 50 minutes without carbidopa. Other labels report up to about 2.3 hours, depending on method.'],
[3,'In Nutt 1984, high-protein meals reversed the benefit of intravenous levodopa without lowering plasma levodopa. What does that show?',['Protein destroys levodopa in the blood','Amino acids compete with levodopa at the blood-brain barrier as well as the gut','Levodopa works only on an empty brain','Protein raises dopamine'],1,'Large neutral amino acids share the carriers levodopa uses in the gut and into the brain. Glycine and lysine, which are not large neutral amino acids, had no effect.'],
[3,'What did ELLDOPA find after its 2-week washout?',['Levodopa groups had worsened most','No difference between groups','Placebo had worsened by 7.8 UPDRS points, the levodopa groups much less','Levodopa caused dyskinesia in everyone'],2,'Placebo worsened 7.8 points versus 1.9, 1.9 and -1.4 on the three doses. Dopamine-transporter uptake declined more on levodopa in the imaging substudy, and the 600 mg arm had more dyskinesia.'],
[3,'What did the LEAP delayed-start trial conclude about levodopa?',['It slows Parkinson\'s disease','It speeds up Parkinson\'s disease','It has no disease-modifying effect','It prevents dyskinesia'],2,'UPDRS change at week 80 differed by 1.0 point (p=0.44), meaning no disease-modifying effect. Dyskinesia and fluctuation rates did not differ.'],
[3,'What is the \'dystonia-improvement-dystonia\' pattern?',['Diphasic dyskinesia','Peak-dose dyskinesia','Off-period dystonia','Freezing of gait'],0,'Diphasic dyskinesia, usually in 15-20% of people, appears as levels rise and fall at the start and end of a dose and mostly affects the legs.'],
[3,'In a pooled literature analysis, about what share had motor fluctuations after 4-6 years of levodopa?',['About 5%','All of them','About 75%','About 40%'],3,'About 40%, with dyskinesia slightly under 40%; fluctuations were nil in the first year. The authors note that clinically important morbidity may be much lower.'],
[3,'In CALM-PD, motor complications at 23.5 months were 28% with pramipexole first. What were they with levodopa first?',['10%','28%','51%','90%'],2,'51% with levodopa (HR 0.45 for pramipexole). Levodopa gave more UPDRS improvement (9.2 versus 4.5 points) and less somnolence.'],
[3,'In the DOMINION study, what share of people taking a dopamine agonist had an impulse-control disorder?',['1.7%','6.9%','17.1%','46.1%'],2,'17.1%, against 6.9% not on an agonist (OR 2.72). Rates were similar on pramipexole and ropinirole.'],
[3,'Which statement about entacapone is in its label?',['It has no antiparkinsonian effect of its own','It is taken once a week','It crosses into the brain and makes dopamine','It needs liver tests every 2 weeks'],0,'Entacapone is given as 200 mg with each carbidopa-levodopa dose and raises levodopa exposure by about 35%. Frequent liver monitoring belongs to tolcapone\'s boxed warning.'],
[3,'Why does tolcapone carry a boxed warning?',['Heart-valve disease','Seizures','Blindness','Fatal fulminant liver failure'],3,'There had been 3 fatal fulminant hepatic failures in more than 40,000 patient-years by May 2005, so the label requires liver enzyme monitoring. Heart-valve regurgitation was a problem with the ergot agonists pergolide and cabergoline.'],
[3,'Which drug is contraindicated with rasagiline?',['Pethidine (meperidine)','Paracetamol','Levodopa','Entacapone'],0,'Pethidine, tramadol, methadone, propoxyphene, other MAO inhibitors, St John\'s wort, cyclobenzaprine and dextromethorphan are contraindicated, with at least 14 days after stopping rasagiline.'],
[3,'According to NICE NG71, when is levodopa offered first line?',['Only after age 70','Never as first line','Only when agonists have failed','When motor symptoms affect quality of life'],3,'When motor symptoms do not affect quality of life, NICE suggests considering a choice of dopamine agonist, levodopa or MAO-B inhibitor.'],
[3,'In Weaver 2009, how much extra on-time without troubling dyskinesia did DBS give, compared with best medical therapy?',['None','About 1 hour a day','About 4.5 hours a day','About 12 hours a day'],2,'DBS gave a mean 4.6 hours a day more versus 0 with medical therapy (difference 4.5). Serious adverse events were more common with DBS, including one death from cerebral haemorrhage.'],
[3,'In the US trial comparing DBS targets (Follett 2010), how did mood differ?',['Depression worsened after STN and improved after GPi stimulation','Depression improved after both','Depression worsened after GPi only','No mood data were collected'],0,'STN needed less medication but had more decline in processing speed. NSTAPS instead favoured STN for off-phase motor benefit, so the choice of target remains debated.'],
[3,'What control signal did adaptive DBS use in ADAPT-PD?',['Heart rate','Wrist accelerometry','Skin temperature','Alpha-beta (8-30 Hz) local field potential power'],3,'Alpha-beta power recorded through the DBS leads. The primary endpoint was revised post hoc, so the authors say confirmatory testing is limited.'],
[3,'In a review of 13,056 DBS patients, how often did intracranial haemorrhage occur?',['2.9% per patient','0.01% per patient','20% per patient','50% per patient'],0,'2.9% per patient (1.6% per lead), half of them symptomatic. All-cause mortality was 0.4%.'],
[3,'Why have focused ultrasound lesions in the pivotal trials been one-sided?',['The scanner can reach only one side','Adjustable stimulation is considered safer for treating both sides','Lesions on the second side are banned worldwide','They work only on the left'],1,'DBS remains the procedure of choice for bilateral treatment because stimulation can be adjusted. Staged bilateral subthalamotomy has been reported in 6 people, and the FDA allows a staged second-side tractotomy.'],
[3,'What did Nutt and colleagues (2010) find about the \'narrowing therapeutic window\'?',['The window narrowed by exactly 50 mg a year','Dyskinesia and the antiparkinsonian response began and ended together','There is no dyskinesia threshold at all','The window widened with age'],1,'In 20 people followed over 4 years, the timings coincided, which the authors judged not consistent with a progressively falling dyskinesia threshold. The idea is widely taught but disputed.'],
[4,'In the PRIAMO study, what share of 1,072 people with Parkinson\'s reported at least one non-motor symptom?',['25%','50%','75%','98.6%'],3,'98.6%, with a mean of 7.8 each. Apathy was the symptom most associated with worse quality of life.'],
[4,'In the Global Parkinson\'s Disease Survey, what was the strongest predictor of quality of life?',['Hoehn and Yahr stage','Depression','Levodopa dose','Age'],1,'Depression. Stage and medication explained only 17.3% of the variation in quality of life.'],
[4,'Which apathy finding supports it as a separate syndrome from depression?',['About half of people with apathy had neither depression nor cognitive impairment','Apathy only occurs with dementia','Apathy is cured by levodopa','Apathy is rarer than 1%'],0,'Pooled apathy prevalence was 39.8%. About half had concomitant depression; the other half had neither depression nor cognitive impairment.'],
[4,'What is the consensus definition of orthostatic hypotension?',['A sustained fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing','Any fall in blood pressure on standing','A fall of 5 mmHg after 10 minutes','A rise in heart rate of 30 bpm'],0,'A heart-rate rise under 15 bpm during the fall suggests a neurogenic cause. In people with supine hypertension, a 30/15 mmHg fall may be more appropriate.'],
[4,'How does the MDS 2019 review rate olanzapine for Parkinson\'s disease psychosis?',['Efficacious and clinically useful','Possibly useful','Investigational','Not efficacious, unacceptable risk, not useful'],3,'Clozapine and pimavanserin are rated efficacious and clinically useful; quetiapine has insufficient evidence but is possibly useful. NICE also says not to offer olanzapine.'],
[4,'In PD COMM, how did standard NHS speech and language therapy compare with no therapy at 3 months?',['It was much better','It did not differ','It was harmful','It was not tested'],1,'LSVT LOUD lowered Voice Handicap Index scores by 8.0 points versus no therapy, while NHS therapy did not differ from no therapy. LSVT LOUD had more adverse events, mostly vocal strain.'],
[4,'How common was dysphagia on objective testing, compared with when people were simply asked?',['82% versus 35%','10% versus 10%','35% versus 82%','50% versus 5%'],0,'Objective testing found 82% (23% in controls), but only 35% reported problems when asked, so swallowing problems are under-reported.'],
[4,'NICE\'s quality standard says adults with Parkinson\'s in hospital take levodopa within how long of the prescribed time?',['5 minutes','15 minutes','30 minutes','2 hours'],2,'30 minutes. The Parkinson\'s Foundation\'s US care summary is tighter, at plus or minus 15 minutes of the home schedule.'],
[4,'In the UK Parkinson\'s Audit summarised by Parkinson\'s UK, what share of people admitted to hospital in 2022 did not get their medication on time every time?',['5%','18%','58%','95%'],2,'58% (50% in 2015, 58% in 2017, 53% in 2019). Missed doses are cited as adding an average of 4 days to a hospital stay.'],
[4,'What is the reported mortality of parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia syndrome?',['About 90%','About 50%','Up to 4%','Zero'],2,'Up to 4%, with about another third of people left with permanent sequelae. It resembles neuroleptic malignant syndrome and can follow abrupt withdrawal or failed absorption of Parkinson\'s medicines.'],
[4,'Which anti-sickness drug do both Parkinson\'s UK and the Parkinson\'s Foundation list to avoid?',['Domperidone','Metoclopramide','Cyclizine','None of them'],1,'Metoclopramide (and prochlorperazine) antagonise dopamine. Parkinson\'s UK says domperidone may be considered without contraindications, but not in cardiac disease.'],
[4,'Why is ondansetron a point of disagreement?',['It blocks levodopa absorption','It causes parkinsonism in everyone','It was withdrawn from the market','It is contraindicated with apomorphine because of profound hypotension and loss of consciousness'],3,'The Parkinson\'s Foundation lists 5HT3 antagonists as safe, but the apomorphine label contraindicates them, so the caution applies to people using apomorphine.'],
[4,'How did a Danish study explain the lower Parkinson\'s risk in smokers?',['Nicotine cures Parkinson\'s','Smoking raises dopamine permanently','Smokers die before diagnosis','Ease of quitting may be an early sign of the disease'],3,'Former smokers who found quitting harder were less likely to develop PD, which the authors read as reverse causation rather than proof that tobacco protects.'],
[4,'Which intervention did a 2016 review consider the only one currently justifiable for primary prevention of Parkinson\'s disease?',['Coffee drinking','Smoking','Promoting physical activity','Raising urate'],2,'Promoting physical activity. Higher moderate-to-vigorous activity was linked to a relative risk of 0.71, and trials of raising urate and of isradipine were negative.'],
[4,'Why was the SURE-PD3 trial of inosine stopped?',['For futility','Because it cured the disease','Because urate did not rise','For lack of funding'],0,'Progression was 11.1 versus 9.9 MDS-UPDRS points a year despite a 44% rise in urate, and kidney stones were more common on inosine.'],
[4,'Did the PADOVA trial of prasinezumab meet its primary endpoint?',['Yes, clearly','Yes, but only in placebo','It was never completed','No: HR 0.84, p=0.066'],3,'Time to confirmed motor progression gave HR 0.84 (95% CI 0.69-1.01). A levodopa-treated subgroup result was exploratory, and the phase 3 PARAISO trial is the confirmatory test.'],
[4,'What did the LUMA trial of the LRRK2 inhibitor BIIB122 report?',['Slower progression in everyone','No effect on progression despite over 90% reduction in a blood LRRK2 marker','It was never started','It cured LRRK2 carriers'],1,'HR 1.04 for confirmed worsening despite over 90% reduction of whole-blood pS935 LRRK2 (as reported from a conference abstract, not yet peer reviewed). Biogen and Denali discontinued BIIB122 in idiopathic PD.'],
[4,'On what evidence did Japan grant conditional approval to an iPSC-derived dopamine-cell product in 2026?',['A 7-patient trial','A 1,000-patient phase 3','Animal data only','A 20-year cohort'],0,'Approval is conditional and time-limited, with 7 years to gather confirmatory data, and applies only in Japan. Conditional approval on 7 uncontrolled patients is not established efficacy.'],
[4,'What did the outpatient palliative care trial (Kluger 2020) find at 6 months?',['No benefit at all','Better quality of life, with no difference in caregiver burden','Worse quality of life','Lower mortality'],1,'Quality of life improved (treatment effect 1.87). Caregiver burden did not differ at 6 months but was lower at 12 months.'],
[4,'In the English trial of community Parkinson\'s nurse specialists, what improved?',['Mortality','PDQ-39','Self-rated global health','Motor test scores'],2,'Mortality, the function tests, PDQ-39 and EuroQol did not change, but self-rated global health was better.'],
[5,'In what year was \'An Essay on the Shaking Palsy\' published?',['1717','1817','1867','1917'],1,'1817, in London. James Parkinson described six cases, several of them people seen casually in the street.'],
[5,'What did James Parkinson write about the mind in the shaking palsy?',['That dementia was universal','That \'the senses and intellects\' were uninjured','That hallucinations came first','Nothing at all'],1,'He held the intellect spared. Later cohorts contradict this: in the Sydney study 83% of 20-year survivors had dementia.'],
[5,'Where did James Parkinson place the cause of the disease?',['The substantia nigra','The basal ganglia','The upper cervical spinal cord, extending to the medulla oblongata','The heart'],2,'He placed it in the \'medulla spinalis\'. He also did not describe rigidity or bradykinesia as cardinal features.'],
[5,'Which features did Charcot add to the clinical picture?',['Bradykinesia and rigidity','Rest tremor and stooped posture','Hallucinations and dementia','Constipation and drooling'],0,'Charcot added bradykinesia and rigidity and named the disease after Parkinson. Parkinson himself had described tremor, stooped posture, drooling and constipation.'],
[5,'Which early treatment did Charcot use?',['Levodopa','Hyoscyamine and other belladonna alkaloids','Deep-brain stimulation','Apomorphine'],1,'Belladonna alkaloids such as hyoscyamine, an early anticholinergic treatment.'],
[5,'Who first described the inclusions now called Lewy bodies, and when?',['Charcot, 1872','Tretiakoff, 1919','Fritz Heinrich Lewy, 1912','Braak, 2003'],2,'Lewy described them in 1912. Tretiakoff\'s 1919 thesis named them \'corps de Lewy\' and described nigral cell loss (Goetz 2011 dates it to 1921).'],
[5,'What did Arvid Carlsson show in 1957-58?',['That levodopa reversed reserpine-induced loss of movement and restored brain dopamine','That MPTP damages the nigra','That alpha-synuclein forms Lewy bodies','That DBS stops tremor'],0,'Reserpine depleted monoamines and stopped spontaneous movement; L-dopa reversed this but a serotonin precursor did not. He shared the 2000 Nobel Prize with Greengard and Kandel.'],
[5,'Who found striatal dopamine depletion in Parkinson\'s brains in 1960?',['Cotzias and Van Woert','Benabid and Pollak','Langston and Ballard','Ehringer and Hornykiewicz'],3,'Ehringer and Hornykiewicz. In 1961 Birkmayer and Hornykiewicz gave intravenous levodopa for the first time.'],
[5,'Which 1967 NEJM report launched high-dose oral levodopa?',['Polymeropoulos and colleagues','Hoehn and Yahr','Spillantini and Goedert','Cotzias, Van Woert and Schiffer'],3,'\'Aromatic amino acids and modification of parkinsonism\', published 16 February 1967. Hoehn and Yahr published their staging scale in the same year.'],
[5,'When did the FDA approve carbidopa-levodopa (Sinemet)?',['1961','1967','1975','1997'],2,'2 May 1975. Levodopa alone (Larodopa) had been approved on 4 June 1970.'],
[5,'What did Langston and colleagues report in 1983?',['Parkinsonism after injecting an illicit pethidine-analogue product containing MPTP','The first DBS operation','The SNCA mutation','The Braak stages'],0,'Four people developed marked chronic parkinsonism, and the authors proposed that MPTP selectively damages substantia nigra cells.'],
[5,'What did Benabid and colleagues report in 1987?',['Fetal cell transplants','Rasagiline synthesis','The first levodopa infusion','High-frequency stimulation of the thalamic VIM nucleus stopped parkinsonian tremor'],3,'100 Hz VIM stimulation stopped tremor, the origin of modern DBS. The FDA approved thalamic stimulation for tremor in 1997.'],
[5,'Why is World Parkinson\'s Day held on 11 April?',['It is the date of Charcot\'s first lecture','It is James Parkinson\'s birthday','It is the date levodopa was approved','It marks the founding of the MDS'],1,'The red tulip, from the \'Dr James Parkinson\' tulip bred in 1980 by J.W.S. Van der Wereld, was adopted as the symbol of PD on 11 April 2005. Some pages give 1981 for the tulip.'],
[5,'Where was the MAO-B inhibitor rasagiline developed?',['At the Technion in Israel','At the Salpêtrière','At the Karolinska Institute','At Queen Square'],0,'Moussa Youdim and John Finberg developed it in the 1970s, and Teva developed it as Azilect. Israel licensed it in January 2005 and the FDA approved it in May 2006.'],
[5,'Which gene carried the first Parkinson\'s mutation found, in 1997?',['LRRK2','GBA1','SNCA (A53T)','PRKN'],2,'SNCA A53T, in an Italian kindred and Greek families. LRRK2 was identified in 2004 and parkin in 1998.']
];
inits.quiz=()=>{
  const F=[['mix','%t:quiz.mixed%'],...Object.keys(PARTN).map(k=>[k,'%t:part%'+k])];
  $('#qFilter').innerHTML=F.map(([k,l])=>`<button class="btn" data-qf="${k}" aria-pressed="${k==='mix'}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  let list=[],i=0,score=0,missed=[],answered=false;
  function start(k){const r=rng(Date.now()%1e6);let L=QZ.filter(q=>k==='mix'||String(q[0])===k).slice();for(let a=L.length-1;a>0;a--){const j=Math.floor(r()*(a+1));[L[a],L[j]]=[L[j],L[a]]}
    list=k==='mix'?L.slice(0,20):L;i=0;score=0;missed=[];q()}
  function q(){answered=false;$('#qBar').style.width=(i/list.length*100)+'%';
    if(i>=list.length){const pct=Math.round(score/list.length*100);
      $('#qCard').innerHTML=`<div class="kicker">%t:quiz.finished%</div><div class="q">%t:quiz.scored{score:score,total:list.length,pct:pct}%</div><p>${pct>=85?'%t:quiz.excellent%':pct>=65?'%t:quiz.solid%':'%t:quiz.retry%'}</p>`+
       (missed.length?`<dl class="kv">${missed.map(m=>`<div><dt>${m[1]}</dt><dd><b>${m[2][m[3]]}.</b> ${m[4]}</dd></div>`).join('')}</dl>`:'')+`<div class="ctrls"><button class="btn primary" id="qAgain">%t:quiz.again%</button><button class="btn" data-go="review">%t:quiz.to_cards%</button></div>`;
      $('#qAgain').onclick=()=>start($('[data-qf][aria-pressed="true"]').dataset.qf);return}
    const Q=list[i];
    $('#qCard').innerHTML=`<div class="readout"><span>%t:quiz.question{n:i+1,total:list.length}%</span><span>%t:quiz.score% <b>${score}</b></span><span>${PARTN[Q[0]]}</span></div><div class="q">${Q[1]}</div><div class="opts">${Q[2].map((o,j)=>`<button class="opt" data-o="${j}"><span class="l">${'%t:quiz.letters%'[j]}</span><span>${o}</span></button>`).join('')}</div><div id="qFb"></div>`;
  }
  $('#qCard').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('.opt');if(b&&!answered){answered=true;const Q=list[i],j=+b.dataset.o,ok=j===Q[3];if(ok)score++;else missed.push(Q);
      $$('.opt',$('#qCard')).forEach((o,k)=>{o.disabled=true;if(k===Q[3])o.classList.add('right');else if(k===j)o.classList.add('wrong')});
      $('#qFb').innerHTML=`<div class="expl"><b style="color:var(${ok?'--good':'--bad'})">${ok?'%t:quiz.correct%':'%t:quiz.wrong%'}</b> ${Q[4]}</div><div class="ctrls" style="margin-top:12px"><button class="btn primary" id="qNext">${i+1<list.length?'%t:quiz.next%':'%t:quiz.results%'}</button></div>`;
      $('#qNext').onclick=()=>{i++;q()};$('#qNext').focus({preventScroll:true})}});
  $('#qFilter').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-qf]');if(!b)return;$$('[data-qf]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===b));start(b.dataset.qf)});
  start('mix');
};

/* =============== 5.4 glossary =============== */
const GL=[
['3-O-methyldopa (3-OMD)','The product of levodopa methylation by COMT, which becomes the main route once decarboxylation is blocked. Its half-life is more than 10 hours, it accumulates, and it may compete with levodopa for transport into the brain.','levodopa'],
['Absolute exclusion criteria','The 9 items in the MDS 2015 criteria that rule Parkinson\'s disease out, such as downward vertical supranuclear gaze palsy or normal presynaptic dopaminergic imaging. They were designed to occur in fewer than 3% of true Parkinson\'s disease.','dx'],
['Adaptive DBS (aDBS)','Closed-loop deep-brain stimulation that adjusts itself to a recorded brain signal, such as alpha-beta local field power. The first commercial system received FDA approval in February 2025.','dbs'],
['Alpha-synuclein','A 140-amino-acid presynaptic protein encoded by SNCA that helps assemble SNARE complexes. Misfolded and aggregated, it is the main component of Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites.','synuclein'],
['Amantadine','A weak NMDA antagonist used for dyskinesia; the extended-release form is also labelled for OFF episodes. Its half-life rises in kidney impairment, and abrupt discontinuation has caused parkinsonian crisis.','drugs'],
['Ambroxol','A drug tested on the GBA1/glucocerebrosidase pathway. An open-label study without a control group raised CSF GCase protein; a placebo-controlled phase 3 (ASPro-PD) is recruiting.','cure'],
['Apathy','Loss of motivation, found in 39.8% of people with Parkinson\'s in a meta-analysis. About half of people with apathy had neither depression nor cognitive impairment.','nonmotor'],
['Apomorphine','A dopamine agonist given by injection, by continuous subcutaneous infusion or (formerly) as a sublingual film. Its label contraindicates ondansetron and other 5HT3 antagonists because of profound hypotension and loss of consciousness.','drugs'],
['Aspiration pneumonia','Pneumonia from food, fluid or saliva entering the airway. In a Korean national cohort it occurred about four times as often in people with Parkinson\'s as in controls (HR 4.21).','nursing'],
['Basal ganglia','Deep brain nuclei (striatum, GPe, GPi, STN and substantia nigra) that form loops with the cortex and thalamus. The motor loop runs through the post-commissural putamen.','circuit'],
['Bemdaneprocel','Dopaminergic progenitor cells grown from human embryonic stem cells. A 12-person phase 1 had no product-related adverse events; a sham-controlled phase 3 (exPDite-2) is recruiting.','cure'],
['Benserazide','A peripheral dopa-decarboxylase inhibitor used with levodopa outside the US, as an alternative to carbidopa. It has no US application.','levodopa'],
['Beta oscillations','Synchronised brain activity in the 13-30 Hz band. Exaggerated beta has been associated with bradykinesia and is reduced by levodopa and by STN stimulation.','circuit'],
['Body-first and brain-first','A hypothesis that Parkinson\'s disease can start in the gut or autonomic nerves and climb the vagus (body-first, often with prodromal RBD) or start in the brain, often on one side (brain-first).','braak'],
['Braak staging','A six-stage model (2003) of Lewy pathology spreading from the lower brainstem and olfactory nucleus through the midbrain to the cortex. Many brains do not follow it, and stage does not track clinical severity.','braak'],
['Bradykinesia','In the MDS definition, slowness of movement AND decrement in amplitude or speed, or progressive hesitations or halts, as movements are continued. It is required for parkinsonism.','dx'],
['Carbidopa','A peripheral dopa-decarboxylase inhibitor that does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It cuts the levodopa needed by about 75% and is saturating at about 70-100 mg a day.','levodopa'],
['Cardiac MIBG scintigraphy','Imaging of the heart\'s sympathetic nerves. Cardiac sympathetic denervation on MIBG is one of the four MDS supportive criteria.','dx'],
['Caregiver burden','The strain of caring. In a UK survey over 40% of caregivers said their health had suffered; burden rose with the person\'s disability, depression, hallucinations, confusion and falls.','living'],
['Cell replacement','Grafting dopamine-producing cells into the putamen. It is not expected to stop the disease process; older sham-controlled fetal-tissue trials missed their primary endpoints.','cure'],
['Clinically established PD','The stricter MDS 2015 level: no absolute exclusions, at least two supportive criteria and no red flags, designed so that at least 90% who meet it have Parkinson\'s disease.','dx'],
['Clinically probable PD','The MDS 2015 level allowing up to two red flags, each counterbalanced by a supportive criterion, with no absolute exclusions. It reached 94.5% sensitivity in validation.','dx'],
['Clozapine','An antipsychotic rated efficacious and clinically useful for Parkinson\'s disease psychosis by the MDS 2019 review, needing specialised monitoring. Low doses (mean 24.7 mg a day) were used in the trial.','nonmotor'],
['COMT inhibitor','A drug that blocks catechol-O-methyltransferase, slowing levodopa breakdown and lengthening its effect: entacapone, opicapone and tolcapone.','drugs'],
['Corticobasal degeneration (CBD)','A disease that can present as corticobasal syndrome (the commonest final diagnosis, 37.1%), a frontal behavioural-spatial syndrome, nonfluent aphasia or a PSP syndrome. A sustained levodopa response is rare.','ddx'],
['DaT-SPECT (DaTscan)','Imaging of the striatal dopamine transporter with 123I-ioflupane. It shows dopaminergic loss but was not designed to distinguish PD, MSA and PSP from one another.','dx'],
['DBS withdrawal syndrome','Rapid, possibly life-threatening worsening when deep-brain stimulation stops, for example when a battery runs out. It may not respond to high-dose levodopa.','nursing'],
['Deep-brain stimulation (DBS)','Implanted electrodes, usually in the STN or GPi, connected to a pulse generator that delivers high-frequency pulses. With the exception of tremor, it treats mainly levodopa-responsive symptoms.','dbs'],
['Delirium','An acute disturbance of attention and awareness. In one prospective study it was diagnosed in 66.9% of hospitalised people with Parkinson\'s, against 38.7% of older controls.','nursing'],
['Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB)','A Lewy body dementia in which dementia starts before or within about a year of parkinsonism (the 1-year rule). Core features are fluctuations, visual hallucinations, RBD and parkinsonism.','ddx'],
['Diphasic dyskinesia','Involuntary movements as levodopa levels rise and fall at the start and end of a dose (dystonia-improvement-dystonia), usually in 15-20% of people and mostly in the legs.','wearing'],
['Direct pathway','The striatum-to-GPi/SNr projection of D1-receptor medium spiny neurons. Activating it releases the thalamus from inhibition and favours movement.','circuit'],
['Directional lead','A DBS lead with segmented contacts around the shaft so current can be steered. In a crossover trial it widened the therapeutic window in 90.6% of patients.','dbs'],
['DJ-1 (PARK7)','A gene whose recessive mutations cause early-onset parkinsonism. Its protein is implicated in the oxidative-stress response.','genes'],
['Dopa decarboxylase','The enzyme that turns levodopa into dopamine. Outside the brain it is blocked by carbidopa or benserazide.','levodopa'],
['Dopamine agonist','A drug that stimulates dopamine receptors directly, such as pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine and apomorphine. Agonists carry a higher risk of impulse-control disorders and sleepiness.','drugs'],
['Dopamine agonist withdrawal syndrome (DAWS)','Anxiety, panic, depression, sweating, pain and drug craving on tapering an agonist, refractory to levodopa. It occurred in 19% of agonist tapers in one series.','drugs'],
['Dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus','A brainstem nucleus that is one of the first sites of Lewy pathology in Braak stage 1. It was spared in 7% of brains in one critical series.','braak'],
['Drug-induced parkinsonism','Parkinsonism caused by dopamine blockers or depleters such as antipsychotics and metoclopramide. It is the second commonest cause of parkinsonism in older people.','ddx'],
['Dyskinesia','Involuntary, often choreic movements that develop with dopaminergic treatment. They can occur at peak dose or in a diphasic pattern.','wearing'],
['Dysphagia','Difficulty swallowing. On objective testing 82% of people with Parkinson\'s have it, but only 35% report it when asked.','nursing'],
['Early-onset Parkinson\'s disease (EOPD)','The MDS term (2022) for onset after age 21 and before 50, preferred to \'young-onset\'. Onset before 21 is called juvenile parkinsonism.','what'],
['ELLDOPA','A placebo-controlled trial of three levodopa doses for 40 weeks plus a 2-week washout. Placebo had worsened most after washout, while dopamine-transporter imaging declined more on levodopa.','levodopa'],
['Entacapone','A COMT inhibitor taken as 200 mg with each levodopa dose. It has no antiparkinsonian effect of its own and lengthens levodopa\'s half-life from 1.3 to 2.4 hours.','drugs'],
['Essential tremor','A bilateral upper-limb action tremor of at least 3 years, without parkinsonism, dystonia or ataxia. A rest tremor added to it is called \'ET plus\'.','ddx'],
['Exenatide','A GLP-1 receptor agonist. A phase 2 trial was positive on motor scores, but the phase 3 Exenatide-PD3 was negative, and The Lancet placed an Expression of Concern on that paper in 2026.','cure'],
['Focused ultrasound (MRgFUS)','MRI-guided ultrasound that makes a small lesion without opening the skull, in the Vim thalamus, GPi or STN. In the pivotal trials it was done on one side only.','dbs'],
['Foslevodopa-foscarbidopa','Prodrugs of levodopa and carbidopa given by 24-hour subcutaneous infusion (Vyalev, FDA 2024). Infusion-site reactions are common.','wearing'],
['Freezing of gait','Brief episodes in which the feet seem stuck. In a survey of 6,620 people, 47% reported regular freezing.','living'],
['G2019S','The commonest pathogenic LRRK2 variant. It increases kinase activity and is especially frequent in North African Arab and Ashkenazi Jewish people with Parkinson\'s.','genes'],
['Gaucher disease','A disease caused by two disease-causing GBA1 variants. Carrying one variant raises the risk of Parkinson\'s disease.','genes'],
['GBA1','The gene for the lysosomal enzyme glucocerebrosidase (GCase). Heterozygous variants raise Parkinson\'s risk with reduced penetrance; it is the commonest genetic finding in panel testing.','genes'],
['Globus pallidus internus (GPi)','An output nucleus of the basal ganglia that inhibits the thalamus. It is a DBS and focused-ultrasound target.','circuit'],
['GLP-1 receptor agonists','Diabetes drugs, including exenatide and lixisenatide, tested for slowing Parkinson\'s disease. Trial results are mixed, and it is unresolved whether positive effects were symptomatic.','cure'],
['Hoehn and Yahr scale','A five-stage scale (1967) from unilateral involvement (1) to wheelchair or bed unless aided (5). It is weighted toward postural instability and says nothing about non-motor problems.','stages'],
['Hummingbird sign','Midbrain atrophy on MRI, with 99.5% specificity but only 51.6% sensitivity for progressive supranuclear palsy.','ddx'],
['Hyposmia','A reduced sense of smell. On age-adjusted testing 74.5% of people with Parkinson\'s had smell loss, and it is predicted to start more than 20 years before diagnosis.','prodrome'],
['Impulse-control disorder (ICD)','Compulsive gambling, sexual behaviour, buying or eating. In DOMINION it affected 17.1% of people on a dopamine agonist versus 6.9% not on one.','drugs'],
['Incidental Lewy body disease','Lewy pathology found at autopsy in people without clinical Parkinson\'s disease. Braak stages 1-2 are thought to be presymptomatic.','braak'],
['Indirect pathway','The route from D2-receptor medium spiny neurons through GPe and the STN to GPi/SNr. Activating it increases inhibition of the thalamus.','circuit'],
['Juvenile parkinsonism','Parkinsonism with onset before age 21. Parkin mutations explained 77% of isolated cases with onset at 20 or younger in one series.','what'],
['Levodopa','The metabolic precursor of dopamine. Unlike dopamine it crosses the blood-brain barrier, using the carrier for large neutral amino acids, and is converted to dopamine in the brain.','levodopa'],
['Levodopa-carbidopa intestinal gel (LCIG)','Levodopa gel infused into the jejunum through a PEG-J tube for about 16 hours (Duopa, FDA 2015). Most adverse events relate to the tube.','wearing'],
['Lewy body','A neuronal inclusion containing alpha-synuclein, first described by Fritz Heinrich Lewy in 1912. Electron microscopy shows crowded membranes, vesicles and organelles.','synuclein'],
['Lewy neurite','Alpha-synuclein-positive pathology in neuronal processes. With Lewy bodies, it is required for the neuropathological diagnosis of sporadic Parkinson\'s disease.','synuclein'],
['Locus coeruleus','A noradrenergic brainstem nucleus involved in Braak stage 2. In one study it lost more neurons than the substantia nigra.','braak'],
['Long-duration response','The sustained benefit from levodopa early in treatment, with little change from dose to dose. In one study it decayed faster over the first 4 years of treatment.','wearing'],
['LRRK2','Leucine-rich repeat kinase 2, the gene behind PARK8. Pathogenic variants increase kinase activity; penetrance is incomplete, and some carriers have no Lewy bodies.','genes'],
['LSVT BIG','A high-amplitude movement training programme of 16 one-to-one hours over 4 weeks. It improved UPDRS motor scores more than Nordic walking or home exercise in one trial.','living'],
['LSVT LOUD','An intensive voice treatment for dysarthria. In PD COMM it improved the Voice Handicap Index more than no therapy or standard NHS speech therapy.','living'],
['MAO-B inhibitor','A drug that blocks monoamine oxidase B, slowing dopamine breakdown: selegiline, rasagiline and safinamide.','drugs'],
['MDS clinical diagnostic criteria','The 2015 Movement Disorder Society criteria: parkinsonism, then 9 absolute exclusions, then 10 red flags counterbalanced by 4 supportive criteria. Built for research, they may guide clinical practice.','dx'],
['MDS-UPDRS','The Movement Disorder Society revision of the Unified Parkinson\'s Disease Rating Scale: 65 items in four parts, each item rated 0 to 4.','stages'],
['Mild cognitive impairment (PD-MCI)','Cognitive decline in Parkinson\'s disease shown on testing but without significant loss of functional independence. Mean prevalence in non-demented people with PD was 27%.','nonmotor'],
['MoCA','The Montreal Cognitive Assessment. In Parkinson\'s disease, a score under 26/30 detected PD-MCI with 90% sensitivity, and under 21/30 detected dementia with 81% sensitivity.','nursing'],
['Motor fluctuations','Swings between ON and OFF periods on treatment, including wearing-off, delayed-on and sudden OFFs. About 40% have them after 4-6 years of levodopa.','wearing'],
['MPTP','A by-product of illicit pethidine (meperidine)-analogue synthesis that caused parkinsonism in people who injected it (1983). It selectively damages nigral cells and became an animal model.','history'],
['Multiple system atrophy (MSA)','An alpha-synuclein disease with autonomic failure plus poorly levodopa-responsive parkinsonism and/or cerebellar ataxia. Stridor is distinctive.','ddx'],
['Neuroleptic malignant-like syndrome','Rigidity, fever, altered consciousness and autonomic instability after Parkinson\'s medicines are reduced or withdrawn, also called parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia syndrome. Reported mortality is up to 4%.','nursing'],
['Nigrosome-1','A region of the substantia nigra seen on iron-sensitive MRI as the \'swallow tail\'. Its loss separated Parkinson\'s disease from healthy adults with 94% sensitivity and 90% specificity.','ddx'],
['Nigrostriatal pathway','The projection of dopamine neurons from the substantia nigra pars compacta to the striatum, which degenerates in Parkinson\'s disease, with the putamen most affected.','circuit'],
['Non-motor symptoms','Problems beyond movement, such as fatigue, anxiety, depression, sleep disorders, constipation, urinary urgency and pain. 98.6% of people in PRIAMO reported at least one.','nonmotor'],
['Normal-pressure hydrocephalus','Enlarged ventricles with gait disturbance, cognitive impairment and urinary problems. Unlike in Parkinson\'s disease, external cues do little for the gait.','ddx'],
['NSD-ISS','The neuronal alpha-synuclein disease integrated staging system (2024), defining disease by synuclein (S) and dopaminergic dysfunction (D) regardless of symptoms. Its authors call clinical use premature.','dx'],
['ON and OFF','ON is the improvement after each dose; OFF is the return of symptoms or lack of levodopa effect.','wearing'],
['Opicapone','A once-daily COMT inhibitor taken at bedtime. In BIPARK-I it reduced off-time more than placebo and was non-inferior to entacapone.','drugs'],
['Orthostatic hypotension','A sustained fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing. Pooled prevalence in Parkinson\'s disease was 30.1%.','nonmotor'],
['Palliative care','Care focused on quality of life and symptoms. Consensus statements support early integration in progressive neurological disease, and NICE suggests considering referral at any stage.','living'],
['Paralysis agitans','An old name for the shaking palsy. Charcot rejected it, noting that people are not markedly weak, and named the disease after Parkinson.','history'],
['Parkinson\'s disease nurse specialist','A nurse with specialist Parkinson\'s training. NICE says regular monitoring, medicine adjustment and a point of contact may be provided by one; trial evidence on outcomes is mixed.','nursing'],
['Parkinsonism','A syndrome defined by the MDS as bradykinesia plus rest tremor and/or rigidity. Parkinson\'s disease is its commonest cause, but drugs, vascular disease and other neurodegenerative diseases can cause it.','what'],
['PDQ-39','A 39-item quality-of-life questionnaire with eight scales: mobility, daily activities, emotional well-being, stigma, social support, cognition, communication and bodily discomfort. Higher scores mean worse function.','living'],
['Peak-dose dyskinesia','Involuntary movements that coincide with the peak of levodopa action; chorea is the commonest form.','wearing'],
['Penetrance','The share of carriers of a variant who develop the disease by a given age. It is incomplete for LRRK2 (about 25-80% by 79-80) and low to moderate for GBA1 (about 8-30% by 80).','genes'],
['PIGD','The postural instability and gait difficulty motor subtype. In DATATOP it carried more impairment than the tremor-dominant subtype.','stages'],
['Pimavanserin','A drug approved by the FDA in 2016 for hallucinations and delusions in Parkinson\'s disease psychosis. It carries a boxed warning about increased mortality in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis.','nonmotor'],
['PINK1','A mitochondrial kinase gene whose recessive mutations cause early-onset Parkinson\'s disease. PINK1 builds up on damaged mitochondria and recruits Parkin to remove them.','genes'],
['Polygenic risk score','A score combining many common risk variants. People in the highest quartile had an odds ratio of 3.74 for Parkinson\'s disease versus the lowest quartile in one cohort.','genes'],
['Prasinezumab','An antibody against aggregated alpha-synuclein. Two phase 2 trials (PASADENA, PADOVA) missed their primary endpoints with exploratory motor signals, and a phase 3 is recruiting.','cure'],
['PRKN (parkin)','The gene behind autosomal recessive juvenile parkinsonism (1998). Parkin carriers often have dystonia at onset, respond well to levodopa and may lack Lewy bodies.','genes'],
['Prodromal Parkinson\'s disease','The stage when early signs of neurodegeneration are present but motor Parkinson\'s disease cannot yet be diagnosed. MDS research criteria estimate it with likelihood ratios.','prodrome'],
['Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP)','A parkinsonian disorder with vertical supranuclear gaze palsy or slow vertical saccades and early unprovoked falls. About a quarter may appear levodopa-responsive.','ddx'],
['Putamen','The part of the striatum that carries the motor loop. Post-mortem putamen dopamine was down 98%, and dorsal putamen dopamine markers were virtually lost from 4 years after diagnosis.','circuit'],
['QUIP','The Questionnaire for Impulsive-Compulsive Disorders in Parkinson\'s Disease, screening for ICDs, other compulsive behaviours and compulsive medication use. A positive screen leads to a full interview.','nursing'],
['Rasagiline','An MAO-B inhibitor developed at the Technion in Israel and by Teva (Azilect). It is contraindicated with pethidine, tramadol, methadone and some other drugs.','drugs'],
['Red flags','The 10 MDS 2015 features of lower specificity that count against Parkinson\'s disease, such as early severe autonomic failure, inspiratory stridor or rapid gait decline. They must be counterbalanced by supportive criteria.','dx'],
['REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD)','A parasomnia of REM sleep, with a pooled prevalence of 46% in Parkinson\'s disease. Confirmed by polysomnography it carries a likelihood ratio of 130 for prodromal Parkinson\'s disease.','prodrome'],
['Rest tremor','In the MDS definition, a 4-6 Hz tremor in the fully resting limb that is suppressed when movement starts. Kinetic and postural tremor do not qualify.','dx'],
['Reverse causation','When an early, unrecognised stage of a disease changes behaviour, making the behaviour look protective. A Danish study read \'ease of quitting\' smoking as an early sign of Parkinson\'s disease.','risk'],
['Rigidity','In the MDS definition, \'lead-pipe\' velocity-independent resistance to slow passive movement. Isolated cogwheeling without lead-pipe rigidity does not qualify.','dx'],
['Rivastigmine','A cholinesterase inhibitor rated efficacious and clinically useful for Parkinson\'s disease dementia in the MDS 2019 review, and indicated for it in the US label.','nonmotor'],
['Rotenone','A pesticide that inhibits mitochondrial complex I. It reproduced nigrostriatal degeneration in rats and was linked to Parkinson\'s disease with an odds ratio of 2.5.','risk'],
['Rotigotine','A dopamine agonist given as a 24-hour skin patch, with sites rotated daily. Parkinson\'s UK guidance uses it as a substitute when people cannot take oral medicines.','nursing'],
['Seed amplification assay (SAA)','A laboratory test that amplifies misfolded alpha-synuclein from a sample such as CSF. It is endorsed for trial enrolment, not approved as a diagnostic test.','dx'],
['Selegiline','An MAO-B inhibitor. In DATATOP it delayed the need for levodopa, but a symptomatic effect could not be excluded.','drugs'],
['Ser129 phosphorylation','Phosphorylation of alpha-synuclein at serine 129: under 4% in healthy brain but over 90% in Lewy bodies.','synuclein'],
['Shaking palsy','James Parkinson\'s name for the disease in his 1817 essay, defined as involuntary tremulous motion with lessened muscular power, a stooped trunk and a tendency to pass from walking to running, \'the senses and intellects being uninjured\'.','history'],
['Short-duration response','The rise and fall of benefit around each levodopa dose, which becomes more important on average after 2-5 years of treatment.','wearing'],
['SNCA','The alpha-synuclein gene on chromosome 4. Point mutations such as A53T and duplications or triplications cause dominant Parkinson\'s disease.','genes'],
['Standardised mortality ratio (SMR)','Observed deaths divided by those expected in the general population. In Parkinson\'s disease it ranged from 1.29 at 10 years to 3.1 at 15-20 years, rising with follow-up.','stages'],
['Substantia nigra pars compacta','The midbrain region whose pigmented dopamine neurons project to the striatum. The lateral ventral tier is the most affected in Parkinson\'s disease.','circuit'],
['Subthalamic nucleus (STN)','An excitatory nucleus in the indirect pathway that becomes overactive when dopamine is lost. It is a main DBS target.','circuit'],
['Supportive criteria','The 4 MDS 2015 features that support Parkinson\'s disease: dramatic dopaminergic response, levodopa-induced dyskinesia, documented rest tremor, and olfactory loss or MIBG denervation.','dx'],
['SWEDD','Scans without evidence of dopaminergic deficit: people diagnosed with Parkinson\'s disease clinically but with normal DaT imaging. Under the MDS criteria a normal scan excludes Parkinson\'s disease.','dx'],
['SynNeurGe','A 2024 research classification based on alpha-synuclein (S), neurodegeneration (N) and genes (G), linked to clinical features. It can classify synuclein-negative, gene-defined disease.','dx'],
['Therapeutic window','The range of levodopa levels that gives benefit without dyskinesia. A progressively narrowing window is widely taught, but one 4-year study found results not consistent with a falling dyskinesia threshold.','wearing'],
['Time-critical medication','A medicine whose dose timing matters clinically. NICE\'s standard is levodopa within 30 minutes of the prescribed time in hospital or care homes.','nursing'],
['Tolcapone','A COMT inhibitor with a boxed warning for fatal fulminant liver failure, requiring liver enzyme monitoring. The Tasmar brand is now discontinued in the US.','drugs'],
['Tremor-dominant subtype','A motor subtype in which tremor predominates. In DATATOP, 441 people were tremor-dominant and 233 PIGD-dominant.','stages'],
['Trichloroethylene (TCE)','An industrial solvent. Ever exposure gave an odds ratio of 6.1 for Parkinson\'s disease in a twin study, with a wide confidence interval because of the small sample.','risk'],
['UK Brain Bank criteria','Three-step clinical criteria (1988-1992): bradykinesia plus another sign, a list of exclusions, and three or more supportive features. NICE NG71 uses them for diagnosis.','dx'],
['Vagotomy','Surgical cutting of the vagus nerve. Danish and Swedish registry studies found lower Parkinson\'s risk after truncal vagotomy in some analyses, but a reanalysis disagreed.','braak'],
['Vascular parkinsonism','Parkinsonism from cerebrovascular disease, often bilateral and \'lower-body\', with a shuffling gait.','ddx'],
['Ventral intermediate thalamus (Vim)','A thalamic nucleus targeted by DBS or focused-ultrasound lesioning to suppress tremor.','dbs'],
['VPS35','A retromer-complex gene whose D620N variant causes dominant, levodopa-responsive Parkinson\'s disease with high but incomplete penetrance.','genes'],
['Wearing-off','The predictable return of symptoms at the end of a levodopa dose; the most frequent type of motor fluctuation.','wearing']
];
inits.glossary=()=>{
  const inp=$('#glSearch');const tname=id=>{const m=MODS.find(x=>x.id===id);return m?m.t:''};
  function render(){const q=inp.value.trim().toLowerCase();const L=GL.filter(g=>!q||g[0].toLowerCase().includes(q)||g[1].toLowerCase().includes(q));
    $('#glList').innerHTML=L.map(g=>`<div><b>${g[0]}</b>${g[1]} <button class="btn ghost" style="padding:3px 9px;font-size:12px;margin-top:6px" data-go="${g[2]}">→ ${tname(g[2])}</button></div>`).join('')||'<p>%t:glossary.none%</p>';
    $('#glCount').textContent=`%t:glossary.count{n:L.length,total:GL.length}%`}
  inp.addEventListener('input',render);render();
};

/* =============== 5.5 sources =============== */
const AREAN={A:'Epidemiology, course, risk & history',B:'Pathology & circuit',C:'Genetics',D:'Diagnosis & differential',E:'Levodopa & medicines',F:'DBS & device-aided therapy',G:'Non-motor, living & caregiving',H:'Nursing care',I:'Research & cure'};
inits.sources=()=>{
  let area='all';const inp=$('#srcSearch');
  $('#srcFilter').innerHTML=[['all','%t:sources.all%']].concat(Object.entries(AREAN)).map(([k,l])=>`<button class="btn" data-sa="${k}" aria-pressed="${k==='all'}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  const esc=s=>s.replace(/&/g,'&amp;').replace(/</g,'&lt;').replace(/>/g,'&gt;');
  function render(){const q=inp.value.trim().toLowerCase();let n=0;
    $('#srcList').innerHTML=SOURCES.map((s,i)=>{const show=(area==='all'||s.areas.includes(area))&&(!q||s.text.toLowerCase().includes(q)||s.key.toLowerCase().includes(q));if(!show)return'';n++;
      return `<li id="src-${s.key}" value="${i+1}"><span class="key">[${i+1}] ${esc(s.key)}${s.aliases?'%t:sources.also%'+s.aliases.map(esc).join(', '):''}</span><span>${esc(s.text)}</span>${s.link?`<a href="${esc(s.link)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${esc(s.link)}</a>`:''}${s.note?`<span class="srcnote">${esc(s.note)}</span>`:''}<span class="mods">${s.areas.map(a=>`<span class="tag sea">${AREAN[a]}</span>`).join('')}${s.unverified?'<span class="tag warn">%t:sources.check%</span>':''}</span></li>`}).join('');
    $('#srcCount').textContent=`%t:sources.count{n:n,total:SOURCES.length}%`}
  inp.addEventListener('input',render);
  $('#srcFilter').addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-sa]');if(!b)return;area=b.dataset.sa;$$('[data-sa]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',x===b));render()});
  render();
};
onShow.sources=()=>{const inp=$('#srcSearch');if(inp&&inp.value){inp.value='';inp.dispatchEvent(new Event('input'))}
  if($('[data-sa="all"]')&&$('[data-sa="all"]').getAttribute('aria-pressed')!=='true')$('[data-sa="all"]').click()};
